item.description:
arXiv:2609.15166v2 Announce Type: replace 
Abstract: For an integer $n > 1$, let $P^+(n)$ be the largest prime factor of $n$, and let $P_y^+(n)$ denote the largest prime factor of $n$ not exceeding $y$. One of Erd\H{o}s and Tur\'an's conjectures asserts that the asymptotic density of integers $n$ satisfying $P^+(n)<P^+(n+1)$ is $1/2$. Furthermore, Wang conjectures that for $x\rightarrow\infty$, one has $\#\{p\leq x:P^+(p-1)<P^+(p+1)\}\sim \pi(x)/2$. In this paper, we show the following results. For any $3\leq y\ll x^{o(1)}$, for $x\rightarrow\infty$, we have \begin{align*}
  &\#\{n\leq x:P^+_y(n)<P^+_y(n+1)\}\sim \frac{1}{2}x, \\&\#\{p\leq x:P^+_y(p-1)<P^+_y(p+1)\}\sim \frac{1}{2}\pi(x), \end{align*} where the first result extends a previous range $3\leq y\ll \exp(\log x/(100\log\log x))$ due to Rivat (2001). For any $0<\alpha<17/32$, let $y=x^\alpha$. Then there exists $h(\alpha)>0$ such that
  \begin{align*}
  \#\{p\leq x:P_y^+(p-1)<P_y^+(p+1)\}\geq(h(\alpha)+o(1))\pi(x).
  \end{align*} In particular, the function $h$ satisfies $\lim_{\alpha\rightarrow 0^+}h(\alpha)=1/2$. Similar result also holds for $\#\{n\leq x:P_y^+(n)<P_y^+(n+1)\}$, which improves a previous result $\lim_{\alpha\rightarrow 0^+} h(\alpha)=1/4$ due to Wang (2019).
